<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">forage</ansi>

Foraging is the skill of gathering useful materials directly from the
environment — herbs, wood, ore, and other raw resources. What you find
depends on where you are and how sharp your eye is.

The biome you're in determines what materials might be available. A forest
yields timber and herbs; a mountainside offers ore and stone; a swamp holds
rare alchemical ingredients.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Governed Skill ━━━</ansi>

The <ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill governs your foraging ability. Your
<ansi fg="yellow">Perception</ansi> stat sharpens your eye. Together, they
determine what quality materials you can find.

At higher <ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill levels, you can uncover
better-quality materials and items that are harder to find. A novice scrapes
together weeds; a skilled forager finds rare roots and choice timber.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ The forage Command ━━━</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">forage</ansi>          Search the current room for harvestable materials.

Some rooms have nothing to forage. Wilderness, dungeons, and natural
environments tend to yield more than city streets or polished floors.

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Connection to Crafting ━━━</ansi>

Materials you gather through foraging feed directly into crafting recipes.
Herbs gathered in the wild become alchemy ingredients; ore and wood become
blacksmithing stock.

You can also find crafting materials by:
  - Picking them up from the floor in crafting rooms.
  - Looting mobs that carry them (cave goblins, feral herbalists, rats).

<ansi fg="yellow">━━━ Skill Progression ━━━</ansi>

Foraging improves through use. Each forage attempt has a chance to advance
your <ansi fg="skill">search</ansi> skill. The higher your rank, the wider
the range of materials you can find.

